    ARBCHAIN* at k = 3 gives [6, 7, 8, 9] — D66's committed 6, 7, 8, 9 exactly, so the corrected blueprint is a strict generalization that AGREES with its parent where the parent was gated.
    ARBCHAIN* at k = 4 gives [8, 10, 12, 14, 16] = k*m + 2*(k-m) = 8, 10, 12, 14, 16: the WHOLE interval [2k, k^2] = [8, 16] is occupied and W4c's k^2 = 16 is SATURATED in a 66-event record over 16 actors.
    D66's OWN `arbchain` at k = 4 gives [8, 9, 10, 11, 12], which is [8, 9, 10, 11, 12] = 3m + 2(k - m) and NOT k*m + 2*(k - m): its secondary arbitrations have THREE proposers whatever k is (its out-degree columns above read 3, never 4).  D66's NOTE and D66's GATE are both k = 3 statements and neither is touched; what is corrected here is the BLUEPRINT's claim to general k.
    AT k = 5 THE CEILING IS NOT REACHED BY EITHER FAMILY: ARBCHAIN* gives [10, 9, 12, 15, 18, 17] against the refined prediction [10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25] and the ceiling k^2 = 25.  The reason is printed in the out-degree/height columns above — the longer bootstrap delivery chains at k = 5 push some depth-1 successors off height + 1, and a chart counts only what sits at EXACTLY height + 2.  So: the k^2 ceiling is REALIZED at k = 3 and k = 4 and UNREALIZED at k = 5 by every schedule this unit built.
  [PASS] K3b THE REFINED BOUND IS EXACTLY RIGHT AT k = 4 AND THE WHOLE INTERVAL [2k, k^2] = [8, 16] IS OCCUPIED BY FORCED, MENU-OFFERED RECORDS — 8, 10, 12, 14, 16 at m = 0..4 — while D66's own ARBCHAIN blueprint, whose docstring claims the same formula for general k, realizes 3m + 2(k - m) instead.  Both families are printed; the correction is to the blueprint's generality, not to any gated D66 number (at k = 3 the two agree exactly: 6, 7, 8, 9)  (ARBCHAIN* meets k*m + 2*(k-m) at every m for k = 3 and k = 4 = True; k = 3 values [6, 7, 8, 9]; k = 4 values [8, 10, 12, 14, 16]; d66's k = 4 values [8, 9, 10, 11, 12] = 3m + 2(k-m) [8, 9, 10, 11, 12]; k = 5 values [10, 9, 12, 15, 18, 17] vs prediction [10, 13, 16, 19, 22, 25]; refusals over all 26 chains = 0)

[K3c WHAT THE SCHEDULE'S PHASE SEPARATION IS WORTH — V1 against V4, the SAME objects with the arbitration order interleaved]
    DOUBLE-GRID(g=3,R=2)             n= 72 max|D| d2= 9 d3= 9 homog d2=0.4444   vs   DGRID-INTERLEAVED(g=3,R=2)       n= 72 max|D| d2= 5 d3= 6 homog d2=0.2361
    DOUBLE-GRID(g=4,R=2)             n=120 max|D| d2=16 d3=16 homog d2=0.4083   vs   DGRID-INTERLEAVED(g=4,R=2)       n=120 max|D| d2= 7 d3= 7 homog d2=0.2583
    READ IT.  V4 has the same actors, the same 2g lineages, the same bootstrap, the same 2g^2 proposals and the same 2g arbitrations per round, and ZERO in-round deliveries — it differs from V1 in NOTHING but the ORDER of the arbitrations inside a round.  Interleaving them destroys the width.  So what buys the k^2 chart is not concurrency alone but the PHASE SEPARATION that puts every row arbitration one layer below every column arbitration of the same round: only then does each proposer register of a row arbitration get consumed by a k-proposer arbitration AT HEIGHT + 1.
  [PASS] K3c THE WIDTH IS A PROPERTY OF THE SCHEDULE'S PHASE SEPARATION, NOT OF CONCURRENCY ALONE, AND THE CONTROL IS A ONE-LINE CHANGE.  Interleaving the row and column arbitrations of a round — same actors, same lineages, same bootstrap, same events, same zero in-round deliveries — collapses max |D| at both g = 3 and g = 4. D66's design finding ('what a second direction needs is a second CONCURRENT consumer') is therefore refined: the consumer must also sit at height + 1  (DOUBLE-GRID(g=3,R=2) 9 -> DGRID-INTERLEAVED(g=3,R=2) 5; DOUBLE-GRID(g=4,R=2) 16 -> DGRID-INTERLEAVED(g=4,R=2) 7)

